# Adding a New RPC Handler

## Step 1 — Ask First
- Read-only query or mutation? (mutations need `requireClaim`)
- Parameters and return type?
- Synchronous response or enqueued command?
- Broadcast to other clients?

## Step 2 — Search
Check `src/rpc/handlers/` for similar methods. Check existing domains:
`frame` · `component` · `connection` · `code` · `game` · `state` · `session` · `items` · `storage` · `patch` · `env` · `power`

## Step 3 — DTO (if new complex return type)
`include/rpc/dto.hpp` declaration + `src/rpc/dto.cpp` implementation.

## Step 4 — Handler
In `src/rpc/handlers/<domain>_handler.cpp`:
```cpp
server.router().on("domain.action", [&server](const Context& ctx, const json& params) -> json {
  requireClaim(server, ctx);  // mutations only
  auto& gm = entt::locator<GameManager>::value();
  if (!gm.started) throw rpc::RpcError{rpc::error::INTERNAL_ERROR, "Game not started"};
  std::lock_guard<std::recursive_mutex> lock(gm.updateMutex);
  // ...
  logWebAction(server, "domain.action", "ok", {});
  return result;
});
```

## Step 5 — Register (new domain only)
Add `rpc::register<Domain>Handlers(rpcServer)` in `src/main.cpp`.

## Step 6 — TypeScript
- Type in `web/src/rpc/types.ts`
- Call in store: `await client.call<T>("domain.action", { params })`

## Step 7 — Verify
`just test-e2e`. Add E2E test to `tests/e2e/`.
